## The chain, in dependency order

**chem/linkhop** — The electron's hop across one link of the net, built from
`chem/bholonomy`'s holonomy amplitude alone, is a 2×2 orthogonal matrix
(entries −1/2 and √3/2). Its two levels are +1 and −1. The electron on a single
link is a two-state system, and the states come from the spin network, not an
assumed tight-binding model.

**chem/vertexhop** — The hop out of a four-valent vertex along each of its four
links. Moving the electron's spin-½ from one pair of links to the other has
coefficient +1 (the 6j symbol with a zero entry, checked against Racah's sum), and
along every link the squared amplitudes sum to one. The hop is defined everywhere
on the net by recoupling theory.

**chem/nethop** — On the diamond net, whose every link is spin ½, the electron
hopping to a neighbour sends the link to spin 1 in two ways (by the neighbour's
inner label), with squared amplitudes 1/4 and 3/4 summing to 1. Once that outcome
is summed, the electron's motion is a unit-amplitude walk. The hop amplitude `t`
is therefore **one number for the whole net**, fixed by the geometry up to the
single overall matter coupling — not a free parameter per bond.

**chem/cover** — The conceptual joint. The hop along a link carries that link's
U(1) holonomy, so it moves the electric flux on the link by one. The electron and
the electric flux it drags are therefore **one walker on the graph's largest
abelian cover**: the state after a walk is the vertex reached together with the
walk's flux chain, and two walks coincide exactly when their chains agree.
Gauss's law holds after every walk (checked on the hexagon and on the smallest
cage of the diamond net); the electric energy Σn² is a potential on the cover.
This is the link between the spin-network side and the electrostatics that earlier
work left unbuilt.

**chem/fluxsplit** — The energy of a whole-number flux splits exactly:
Σn² = Σh² + Σ(n−h)², where Σh² is the charges' own Coulomb part (`chem/charge`'s
`field-energy`, the resistance-metric energy) and n−h ranges over one coset of the
integer circulations — the class of the charges in the graph's finite abelian group
(order = the spanning-tree count). With the electric term alone the energy between
two charges is a string that grows with their distance (the shorter arc on a ring).
Foster's theorem is checked on the hexagon, the cage, and the complete graph.

**chem/sectors** — In QSD V's regularization the magnetic loops at a vertex move no
flux between vertices (625 flux sectors checked), so the energy at a vertex is a
function only of the charge there. Consequently these terms carry **no force
between charges at different vertices**: a force requires the graph-preserving loop
operator, not the local vertex terms. This is why the string, not a Coulomb law,
is what the local electric term produces.

**chem/netpiece** — The Coulomb part on the diamond net closed with period two —
sixteen vertices, which is exactly the graph of the 4-cube. The charges' energy at
one to four lines of separation is 15/32, 7/12, 61/96, 2/3, computed by solving
Kirchhoff's equations over the rationals. These were derived independently from the
group characters and the machine agrees.

**chem/ringatom, chem/netatom** — The atom on a ring and on the net, with the hop
`t`, the Coulomb scale λ, and the loop coupling K left free. On the cover the
Hamiltonian is H = (λ/2)·(flux energy) − t·(hop) − K·(ring loops), and its moments
at the nucleus are checked as polynomial identities in t, K, λ.

**chem/ringbound** — The two-sided bound on a ring, made exact and finite:
(λ/2)·R − 2t ≤ E₀ ≤ (λ/2)·R, where R = d(N−d)/N is `chem/charge`'s effective
resistance (checked to equal N⁻¹·d(N−d) on every ring up to eight vertices). This
closes the lower bound on the Coulomb energy **without the dual-torus argument**
that was the only prior route — a bracket of width 2t, from the flux side alone.

**chem/cwbound** — The same lower bound on the net, for free t and λ, by the
discrete Collatz–Wielandt principle: for any positive trial vector,
min_u (Hψ)_u/ψ_u ≤ E₀ ≤ max_u (Hψ)_u/ψ_u. The potential extremes and the vertex
degrees are checked, giving a finite rigorous bracket on the bond's depth with no
eigenvalue asserted by hand.

### H₂⁺ — two nuclei, one electron

A lone ion has net charge, so its field must terminate; the derivation places it on
a piece of the net with a wall that carries the flux out (`chem/h2plus-net`). The
potentials are given and checked against Kirchhoff's law at every vertex.

- **chem/h2plus-net** — At t = 0 the electron sits on a nucleus, that site is
  neutral, and the energy is independent of nuclear separation: a neutral atom and
  a bare nucleus feel no force. To second order in t, two nuclei sharing a common
  neighbour gain more than two nuclei on opposite sites.
- **chem/bondpoly** — The opposite-placement bond operator's characteristic
  polynomial is exactly p_f = x(x−118)(x−140) − s(6x−280), s = (280t/λ′)², proved by
  machine (closing a hand reduction). The opposite placement's energy is the least
  root of p_f exactly, not a bound.
- **chem/bondsep, chem/bondcross** — The bonding (shared-neighbour) placement is
  **strictly lower** than the alternative at every coupling checked from t = 0
  through t/λ′ ≈ 0.19, by an integer root-bracketing certificate, with no crossover
  in the regime where the reduction is controlled. (An earlier hand estimate that the
  opposite placement wins at large t was wrong and is withdrawn.)

### H₂ — two nuclei, two electrons

Two protons and two electrons are neutral, so the molecule lives on a closed piece
with no wall (`chem/h2-net`, `chem/h2two`).

- **chem/h2-net** — Electrostatically, the ground is two neutral atoms (electrons on
  the protons, energy zero at any separation); every charge-separated configuration
  costs. Binding is therefore not electrostatic. The shared bond vertex admits only
  the spin singlet.
- **chem/h2two** — **H₂ binds for every hop amplitude t > 0.** The separated-atom
  state has zero Coulomb energy and zero diagonal (no electron can hop without
  leaving a proton), while its four one-hop neighbours each cost 3/4; the 2×2
  effective Hamiltonian [[0, −2t], [−2t, 3λ′/4]] has determinant −4t² < 0, so its
  lowest eigenvalue is negative at every coupling. The binding is purely the hop's —
  the off-diagonal against the separated state's zero diagonal.

## The single external input

Both molecules' binding is set by two couplings: the hop t (fixed by the geometry,
`chem/nethop`) and the ratio K/λ of the magnetic to the electric coupling. From
QSD V the Maxwell Hamiltonian is q_ab/(2Q²√det q)·[E² + B²] with canonical pair
(A, E/Q²); because the quantized momentum is E/Q², the integer charge labels are
fluxes of E/Q², so the electric coupling λ ∝ Q² ∝ α and the magnetic K ∝ 1/Q² ∝ 1/α.
Hence **K/λ = c_geom · (1/α²)**, with c_geom the geometry's contribution and α the
fine-structure constant.

**chem/cgeom** — c_geom is the rational **−3/8**, proved. Both couplings are traces
of the same operator A_e = √V − M_e at the four-valent vertex. The electric cost per
unit flux is a − b = ¼·3^{1/4}(1 − 5^{1/4})² and the magnetic self-term is
K₀₀ = −(3/32)·3^{1/4}(1 − 5^{1/4})². The radical — carrying the volume eigenvalue √3
and the invariant space's 5^{1/4} — is common to both, so it cancels:
K₀₀/(a − b) = −3/8. Checked as 8·K₀₀ + 3·(a − b) = 0 in closed form (no division).

**Universality.** −3/8 is not an accident of the all-½ spectrum. It follows from two
theorems the module already proves: (a − b) + 2(K̄₀₀ − K̄₀₁) = 0 (the electric cost
is twice the magnetic triad difference), and K̄₀₀ + 3K̄₀₁ = 0 (the four edges are
equivalent, so the triads close — this is the classical tetrahedral angle
K₀₁/K₀₀ = −1/3). Substituting gives a − b = −(8/3)K̄₀₀, hence −3/8, with the volume
scale never entering. So **the geometry's contribution to K/λ is a pure rational at
every symmetric four-valent vertex**, whatever its spin; the irrationalities of the
quantum geometry cancel between the magnetic and electric terms.

## What is established, and what is not

Established, as checked programs: the full chain above — the hop and the flux unified
on the cover, the Coulomb energy as a resistance metric, the no-force result for the
local terms, the two molecules' binding, and K/λ = (−3/8)·(geometry)/α² with the
geometric rational proved and shown universal.

Not established, and not to be claimed as a closed number: the overall dimensionful
prefactor of K/λ — the Planck-length powers and Thiemann's regularization constants —
is convention-dependent bookkeeping that has not been read off line-by-line from
QSD V. It does not affect any result above, but no single numeric value of K/λ should
be asserted until it is pinned. Likewise, α itself is an input to the matter action,
not predicted; no quantization of geometry fixes it, and this chain does not claim to.

The honest one-line summary: the chemistry of H₂⁺ and H₂ is derived from quantum
geometry down to a single constant — the fine-structure constant — with a clean
rational, −3/8, wherever the geometry speaks.

